hey guys pete here fargo season 4 has  come to an end  a history lesson on the alternate  economy of kansas city set the stage for  a full-blown gang war  that consumed the city as 1950 gave way  to 1951.  as we've come to expect from fargo it  has no shortage of interesting  characters  career criminals law enforcement  officers  and of course some civilians who find  themselves caught up in the action  season 4 gave us a whole gang of  underdogs  a failed son who finds himself in charge  of his connected mafia family  an angel of death who has an affinity  for baking  a chatty mormon us marshall who's earned  the nickname  deffy because he only hears what he  wants to hear a duo of escaped convicts  who fancy themselves as  outlaws rather than criminals a student  who happens on her next-door neighbor's  deadly secret  and a local cop who the u.s military  thought would make a great minesweeper  even though he's plagued with nervous  ticks fargo's fourth season  also gave us the origin story of a fan  favorite character from season 2  and the showrunner has provided some  additional context for that  in his post-finale interviews which i'll  get into in a little while  before we begin as is tradition this is  your spoiler warning  if you haven't watched all 11 episodes  of fargo season 4 then this video will  absolutely spoil it  my goal here is to explain the ending  after all i'm also going to talk about  some of the previous seasons  so if you're a late comer to the series  it's something to think about however i  don't think that will spoil those  seasons any more than watching season 4  would  with that out of the way let's get  started if you followed along with my  season 4 episode recap and reviews  you know i had some problems with this  season compared to the previous  installments  at the same time it had some great ideas  and some great characters  all served up in a great looking period  piece that boasted some gorgeous  cinematography  so today i just wanted to talk about the  ending what noah holly might have been  trying to say  what the ending means for kansas city  and its characters  what the ending means for the rest of  the series and in the end  what's worth remembering fargo always  features at least one character that  could be described as inherently good  you've got all these criminal types  running around doing their thing  and on the other side you've got your  lou and molly solversons  and your gloria burgles these are  usually cops  they're usually white and so season four  changes this  up our moral center this time around is  ethel rita pearl smutny a biracial teen  whose parents find themselves  caught up with the local black criminal  outfit their attempt to pay off their  debt to lloyd cannon  using money that zelmer and swaney stole  from him  creates a situation where he takes over  their family business  in the end it's ethereda's wits that  help her family come away with a happy  ending  she uses donatello fada's ring which she  found in orienta mayflower's apartment  to broker a deal with loy cannon and  help him win his war  in the 10th episode we see her pick up a  pair of suitcases and leave in the  finale and the showrunner has confirmed  that she makes it out in the end he said  that her reward for surviving the story  is that she goes off and has an  unremarkably positive life she's going  to move to paris she's going to become a  writer and she's going to prosper  outside of america she knows in that  moment that she can't prosper inside  america  and she has the wherewithal to get out  and the season as a whole fits into her  school history report which the season  opens  and closes with i'll get into that  history report at the end but for now  let's look at the different sides of the  gang war  and how things played out for each side  in the season finale  josto finds himself as the boss of the  foda family  after he makes an offhanded comment  about his father  to a nurse that he got some drugs off of  she turned out to be a serial killer  she killed his father and since their  outfit is structured as a family  business  that means he's now in charge he's not  really the right guy for the job  by the time we reach the finale that  ring i mentioned that ethel rita gave  over to lloyd cannon  is now in the hands of the family's  concierie  who accuses him of killing his father in  order to take over the business  and they've bailed his accomplice out of  jail who's more than happy to cop to the  murder  josto isn't a sympathetic character he's  pretty much an incompetent arrogant ass  when it comes down to it but that isn't  all there is to his story  after all he was traded away as a child  just like satchel and rabbi  and we know he was the victim of abuse  in that situation  in the end he did start the whole gang  war but it was by accident  and abel used that opportunity to his  own advantage  his story winds up being tragic in that  he reconciles with his brother  only to see him die shortly afterward  and then his own people take him down  for the crime of taking power for  himself with abel using that as a  vehicle to do the exact same  thing orienta's confession is enough to  earn them both a bullet in the head  and she manages to salt his wounds by  requesting that he's killed first so  that she can watch him die  before he goes he makes the case that  his own men killing him is what they  want us to do  it's a ladder but there's nowhere to go  and that is where he found himself he  made it to the top  and wound up at the bottom of an  unmarked grave in the middle of nowhere  this did make for my favorite sequence  of the finale  and that had a lot to do with my  favorite character of season 4 orienta  mayflower  we see that she does enjoy watching this  lover of hers die  and when it's her turn she doesn't seem  all that worried about it  there was one curious thing here that i  missed in my previous video  and that's that when she's looking at  her reflection we also see the  reflection of the ghost  aka mr snowman aka captain  roach i don't have a great explanation  for why the ghost is now following her  but this could be a bright spot  considering that this might mean  that he doesn't follow the smutney  family anymore  on the canon side of things loy almost  makes it out  on top he's able to dispatch leon and  his uncle happy  who double-crossed him siding with josto  he finds out that his son  who he traded for power who he thought  had died  did manage to make it home at this point  all the work and all the sacrifices seem  to have paid off  and then things take a turn when he  realizes that abel vellante  who's now in charge of the family won't  honor their deal  it's a situation where abel knows he has  the power behind him  so he sees no reason to give anything to  loy  this is something we'd seen throughout  the season in episode 1 ethel rita  described it like this  whoever was last off the boat finding  the doors of honest capital clothes  rolled up their sleeves and got to work  getting rich the old-fashioned way  so these different ethnic criminal gangs  essentially turn to crime  as a way to make a living as immigrants  they're all seen as outsiders  but loy and his people are in a  different situation  because they're black they'll never be  able to assimilate into that society  in the same way and we saw this with the  credit card he had a great idea  we all know how that would turn out but  rather than cut him in  the bank manager just took his idea and  made it his own  with no real fear of that coming back on  him so in the finale we see loy cut out  we see him lose all his power  and then we see him return home seeing  his family together  and having that moment of clarity where  he realizes he may not get that but he  does have this  in noah halley's words loy realizes i  thought i needed more power to keep him  safe and now that i have no power  no one is after us and maybe this is a  good thing he has this moment where he  thinks maybe this is a happy ending  and then of course we know how that ends  zelmer roulette  returns to avenge her lost lover swanny  capps  she stabs loy multiple times and he dies  and what's interesting about that is  that she does this in retaliation  he gave them train tickets he sent them  away and then he went back on  his word he told the cops where they  were going  and so as it usually does in this  universe the things you do can come back  to you  and this one decision does him in  i mentioned him trading his son for  power the idea there that we saw had  been going on for generations without  much luck  was that each family would trade a son  and that would prevent the escalation of  hostilities  when josto was going to break that deal  and have satchel killed rabbi milligan  swooped in to save him  before he was carried away to his end by  a tornado  he'd been taking the majority of his  time taking care of satchel  and preparing him for the world his  motivation was that he wanted  satchel to have a choice rabbi was made  a child soldier  as were many of the kids in these  families they didn't have a say in it  so when the war breaks out he takes him  away leaving satchel on his own with  this new dog rabbit that he found in  their hotel  ultimately he decides to go home to his  family where he gets a brief reunion  that ends in watching his father die  right in front of him  now i mentioned that ethel rita and her  family made it out with a happy ending  and as the story teller the one that  explained this world that these  characters inhabit and the things that  they're up against  i think she wraps it up really nicely in  the ending of her report  she says history is a form of memory but  what does it mean to remember  ask yourself who writes the books who  chooses what to remember  and what gets forgotten and damn it if  it isn't satchel  aka mike milligan this is his memory  this is what we see  him thinking about when we see the  images overlapped where we see him  riding down the road  we see the image of satchel and rabbit  making their way  thinking about rabbi who always warned  him if he didn't return then he was  either dead or in jail  we see mike milligan taking the clip out  of his gun and putting in the next one  in the exact same way we saw a rabbi  doing that earlier in the season  and we see that all of this that's  happened satchel cannon slash mike  milligan  he's the one that carries the memory  when we meet him again in 1979  we see that he ends up working for the  organization that took his father down  of course he knows it was zelmer that  actually killed him but his conflict  with the fadas is what brought him to  that place  again to reference the showrunner when  asked about what happens between satchel  being on the porch and watching his  father die  in the moment where we meet mike years  later he said his feeling was that the  family wasn't able to stay in that house  their source of revenue dried up after  lloyd died  that they inevitably went through some  hard times in that then  ultimately satchel didn't have a lot of  opportunity in his life  and he had to go back to those skills he  learned from rabbi  he said that on some level the moment  his dad was killed in front of him he  didn't really have that choice that  rabbi wanted him to have  anymore and that he had to change his  name because being a cannon wasn't a  safe thing to be  so he chose the milligan name then at a  certain point having laundered his  identity he started pulling jobs of some  kind  and ended up in the kansas city mafia  which is ultimately what became of the  thought of family business when abel  took over  in a different interview when asked why  he wanted to tell mike milligan's story  holly said i suppose on some level the  story is an attempt to explain the  origins of someone as unique as him  the idea that he's canon's son but  raised by a second father  who himself was raised by irish and  jewish and italian parents  and on some level that's the history of  america right there  in season two we also see what becomes  of abel's vision  we see joe bulow still in a role with  the organization and we see his  interactions with the gerhart family he  mentions the problems he has with the  family business  and now we know where those beliefs came  from it's all a reflection of the bigger  picture  in the 1950s all these gangs of others  were stirred up fighting against each  other rather than the system that put  him in that position  as time goes on power's consolidated and  smaller outfits are absorbed like all  the mom and pop shops that go out of  business when walmart moves in  there really was no place for the cannon  syndicate in that new kansas city  we also see what's to become a mort  kellerman after he killed josto and  gautano's mother  and it will be interesting to watch it  again with all this new information  and with that i think i've touched on  all the things i wanted to when i set  out to make this video today  if you're looking for a more in-depth  breakdown and critique i did all the  episodes except number seven in this  season  and in my finale video i gave my overall  review for the season  i will say that after reading all these  interviews  it does sound like there's a really good  chance we'll get more fargo in the  future  at least one more season no guarantees  there of course but  it's clear there's interest when you  hear him talk about this story  i think i probably have one more fargo  video coming this weekend  i'm thinking of doing one where i look  at all the different connections across  the seasons  so please like this video if you enjoyed  it please subscribe to my channel if you  haven't already  thanks for watching i'll talk to you  soon
 

